\section{Introduction}
Modern intrusion detection systems (IDS) face increasing challenges due to the rapid evolution of cyberattacks, including distributed denial-of-service campaigns, botnets, and adaptive malicious traffic \cite{buczak2016survey}. Traditional signature-based IDS methods often fail to detect previously unseen attacks \cite{sommer2010outside}. Although machine learning and deep learning approaches have improved detection performance \cite{vinayakumar2019deep,ferrag2020deep}, many methods suffer from high computational complexity, lack of interpretability, and poor generalization.

This work proposes a fundamentally different perspective based on coherence geometry. Instead of directly learning decision boundaries in high-dimensional spaces, Vupr-QLI constructs a complex-valued coherence representation:
\begin{equation}
Q = x + iy
\end{equation}
where $x$ describes coherent transport structure and $y$ represents instability-related fluctuations. The central hypothesis is that malicious traffic perturbs the coherence geometry of network-flow distributions, enabling linear separation in the complex plane.

\section{Related Work}
\subsection{Intrusion Detection Systems}
IDS are generally classified into signature-based, anomaly-based, and hybrid systems \cite{ahmad2021machine}. Machine learning methods such as Random Forests \cite{breiman2001random}, Support Vector Machines, and deep models like LSTMs \cite{yang2023hierarchical} and autoencoders \cite{wang2024dual} have demonstrated strong performance on benchmark datasets including CIC-IDS2017 \cite{sharafaldin2018toward}. However, these methods often require extensive training and large computational resources.

\subsection{Complex-Valued Representations}
Complex-valued neural networks naturally encode amplitude and phase information \cite{hirose2012complex,trabelsi2018deep}, making them suitable for signal processing tasks. Vupr-QLI extends these concepts through a physically interpretable coherence manifold derived from network-flow statistics, avoiding the training overhead of deep networks.

\section{CIC-IDS2017 Dataset}
The experiments use CIC-IDS2017 \cite{sharafaldin2018toward}, developed by the Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity. The dataset includes BENIGN traffic, DoS attacks, DDoS attacks, Port scans, Botnet traffic, Web attacks, and Infiltration attacks. This study focuses on Monday and Wednesday captures, analyzing the DoS Hulk attack class versus BENIGN traffic.

\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Data Preprocessing}
Infinite and missing values are removed: $X \rightarrow X_{clean}$. Feature normalization uses z-score normalization:
\begin{equation}
z = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Coherence Features}
The coherent transport coordinate is defined as:
\begin{equation}
x = \frac{1}{N_c}\sum_{i=1}^{N_c} c_i
\end{equation}
using Flow Duration, Total Length of Fwd Packets, and Total Length of Bwd Packets.

The instability coordinate is defined as:
\begin{equation}
y = \frac{1}{N_i}\sum_{i=1}^{N_i} u_i
\end{equation}
using Flow Bytes/s, Flow Packets/s, Fwd Packet Length Std, and Bwd Packet Length Std.

\subsection{Complex Coherence Space}
The Vupr-QLI coherence operator is:
\begin{equation}
Q = x + iy
\end{equation}
with coherence magnitude:
\begin{equation}
|Q| = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}
\end{equation}
and phase:
\begin{equation}
\phi = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{y}{x}\right)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Projected Coherence Score}
The one-dimensional coherence projection is:
\begin{equation}
S = |Q|\cos(\phi - \theta_0)
\end{equation}
with $\theta_0 = \frac{\pi}{4}$. This projection enhances separability between benign and malicious traffic by aligning the decision boundary with the dominant attack direction in the complex plane.

\section{Experimental Setup}
Balanced sampling was applied: 10,000 BENIGN and 10,000 DoS Hulk flows. The primary metric is the Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC):
\begin{equation}
AUC = \int_0^1 TPR(FPR)d(FPR)
\end{equation}
Baselines include Isolation Forest \cite{liu2008isolation}, LOF \cite{breunig2000lof}, Random Forest \cite{breiman2001random}, LSTM-Attention \cite{yang2023hierarchical}, and Dual-AE \cite{wang2024dual}.

