\section{Discussion}
The results support the central hypothesis: malicious traffic perturbs the coherence geometry of network flows. Unlike black-box models, Vupr-QLI provides geometric interpretability via phase-space analysis. The framework achieves 120,000 flows/sec on a single CPU core, versus ~6,500 for LSTM-Attention, making it suitable for real-time SOC deployment. While deep models \cite{yang2023hierarchical,wang2024dual} achieve competitive AUC, they lack the direct interpretability and CPU efficiency of Vupr-QLI.

\section{Limitations}
Several limitations remain: (1) Only DoS Hulk was analyzed; multi-class evaluation is ongoing. (2) Feature selection remains static. (3) Real-time deployment requires further engineering.


\section{Conclusion}
This paper introduced Vupr-QLI, a coherence framework for intrusion detection using complex-valued network-flow representations. By mapping traffic statistics into $Q = x + iy$, we achieve 0.947 AUC on CIC-IDS2017 DoS Hulk with CPU-only inference. The method offers interpretable geometric structure, efficient anomaly discrimination, and compatibility with modern IDS architectures, opening new directions for coherence-based cybersecurity analytics.

